    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (3/3 passing, BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added an optional, validated-when-present `postcode` to the owner, keeping the request contract backward-compatible.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **OpenAPI spec** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added an optional `postcode` field to `OwnerFields` with `pattern: '^[0-9]{4}$'`. Since `performBeanValidation` is on, this makes a malformed postcode (e.g. `\"12\"`) a Bean Validation failure \u2192 400. It's absent from `required`, so owners without a postcode stay valid. Being on `OwnerFields`, it's both accepted on create and returned on read (via the `Owner` `allOf`).\n\n2. **`Owner` entity** \u2014 new `postcode` column + getter/setter.\n\n3. **Schema SQL** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added a `postcode` column to the `owners` table (tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the DDL was required).\n\n4. **`PostcodeValidator` + `InvalidPostcodeException`** (util) \u2014 the validator derives the region from the city via the existing `LocalityResolver` and checks the fixed ranges (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099). A `null` postcode is accepted; a city with no known region accepts any 4-digit postcode; an out-of-range postcode throws.\n\n5. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 maps `InvalidPostcodeException` to 400, mirroring the existing `InvalidTelephoneException` handler.\n\n6. **`OwnerRestControllerV1`** \u2014 calls `PostcodeValidator.validate(city, postcode)` on both create and update (and update now persists the postcode). MapStruct auto-maps the matching `postcode` property both directions.\n\nThe region-range table is duplicated in the validator rather than shared with `LocalityResolver`, which still only knows city\u2192region \u2014 consistent with how the codebase keeps those lookups separate. One note: validation order in `addOwner` runs postcode-range before the capacity/duplicate checks, so an out-of-range postcode yields 400 even if the request would also hit a 429/409; the spec didn't pin that ordering, and input validation first is the conventional choice.",
