    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the codebase's existing conventions:\n\n- **`PostcodeValidator`** mirrors the established `util` helper pattern (`TelephoneNormalizer`, `AddressNormalizer`, `LocalityResolver`): `final` class, private constructor, static entry point, static lookup table. It correctly **reuses `LocalityResolver.resolve()`** rather than duplicating the city\u2192region table \u2014 the region-keyed design is the right unit for the postcode\u2192region table.\n- **`InvalidPostcodeException`** faithfully parallels `InvalidTelephoneException` (a `RuntimeException` in `util`), and the new `ExceptionControllerAdvice` handler mirrors the existing telephone handler one-for-one (same `detailBuild`/`ERROR_INVALID_REQUEST`/400 shape). No catch-all growth there.\n- Cross-field validation (city + postcode together) genuinely can't ride the mapper's per-field `@Named` methods, so invoking it at the controller boundary is the natural fit, not a misplacement.\n- The `addOwner` (validates the mapped `owner`) vs `updateOwner` (validates the `ownerFieldsDto` it copies from) asymmetry simply tracks the controller's **pre-existing** style \u2014 `updateOwner` already copies/transforms fields inline (`ownerMapper.normalizeTelephone(ownerFieldsDto...)`) while `addOwner` leans on the mapper. Each method validates the object it is about to persist, so this is coherent rather than inconsistent.\n- Schema (all four DB dialects), model field, and OpenAPI additions are standard and complete; the `v2` controller only lists owners, so there's no add/update path left unguarded.\n\nNothing duplicated, nothing becoming a catch-all, logic lives in sensible units \u2014 a maintainer here would find it natural.",
